{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-95143",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,i-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,j+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡z\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y={t}^{2}$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}\\,k)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
